      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think both failures described here are already fixed by aosp/4368058 and aosp/4368055, which this patch sits on. aosp/4368058 bounds these mmaps with `vma_kernel_pages()`, which counts from slice `vm_slice_off`, so a whole-buffer 4KB mapping is accepted. And since aosp/4368055, `remap_pfn_range()` starts at the slice that `vma_slice_offset()` gives `vm_start`, which is slice `vm_slice_off` of page `pfn + vm_pgoff`.\n\nAs far as I can see, `p3s_mmap_phys_object()` always takes the fast path of `p3s_remap_phys_range()` here: `PFN_PHYS(pfn)` is 16KB aligned, so `phys + vma_file_offset(vma)` starts at slice `vm_slice_off`, and the call is the same `remap_pfn_range(vma, vma-\u003evm_start, pfn + vma-\u003evm_pgoff, vma_size(vma), ...)` as without this patch. The only change I can find is the limit, which drops from `PAGE_ALIGN(size)` to `size` rounded up to 4KB. The bytes in between are the rest of the buffer\u0027s last kernel page, which belongs to the allocation and which a native process can map too. The `dma_mmap_p3s`, `dma_mmap_full_p3s` and `iommu_dma_mmap_p3s` tests in aosp/4367986 use a 16KB buffer, where both limits are the same.\n\nWould it be OK to drop this patch? If you have a case that still fails without it, could you share it? Then we could fix it in `remap_pfn_range()` or `vma_kernel_pages()` for every caller.",
      "isAi": true,
